## 4. Effect on the candidates

Precise about eliminated vs. deprioritized, as asked:

**delete-entirely — eliminated as a default; not formally forbidden.** — **Moderate-High.**
The person did not say "don't delete it." They said it is worth using. But a panel that carried deletion forward as a live recommendation after this answer would, in my reading, be recommending against a preference the person just volunteered without being asked. I would treat it as off the table unless the person reopens it, and I would say so to them in those terms rather than quietly dropping it. If my "chứ" reading in §0 is wrong, this downgrades to "strongly deprioritized."

**stay-thin-and-fix — survives, but its justification has been swapped underneath it and "fix" no longer has a referent.** — **Moderate-High.**
Its old warrant was "the shell is alive and needs care." The new situation is "the shell is wanted and unwatched." Those recommend different work. More pointedly: *fix what?* No one in this session, as far as I know, has established that anything is broken. The candidate now presupposes a diagnosis nobody has made. It needs re-specification before it can be compared against the others.

**stay-thin-smaller-path — strengthened, on the attention constraint.** — **Moderate.**
"Nhiều việc quá" is a direct argument for the smallest viable path, from the person's own mouth. This is the candidate whose case improved most. I am recording that it improved; I am not selecting it.

**A gap none of the three cover, which I raise as shaped-work rather than as a choice:** all three candidates assume the shell's current condition is known. It is not. Whether the shell still runs against the current daemon is (a) decision-relevant to all three candidates, (b) answerable by execution rather than by asking the person, and (c) plausibly cheap. Both shapers skipped it because both believed they already knew why the commits stopped — Reading A thought it was dead, Reading B thought it was fine, and neither needed to check. That shared premise is now gone. — **Moderate-High.**

I am not proposing this as a fourth candidate and I am not ranking it. I am noting that the evidence base under the existing three has a hole in it that this turn exposed and that a machine, not the person, can fill.

